# Arccross Combat Economy — Implementation Walkthrough

The structural rules for the Arccross Combat Economy have been implemented across the entire core system. The codebase is now prepared to handle the precise AP management and dynamic reaction pipelines specified.

## Core Architectural Changes

> [!NOTE]
> The biggest shift is the **Reaction Window System**. Defensive actions now intercept standard linear flow without relying on spaghetti code, utilizing a clean signal-driven architecture that naturally hooks into your animation event players.

### The AP Economy & Stance Scale
*   **12-Point Baseline**: The `CombatTurnManager` now strictly enforces the 12 AP cost ledger for actions.
*   **Felled & Prone Recovery**: When a unit reaches 0 Stance Points, they enter `FELLED`. At the start of their next skipped turn, they auto-recover 6 Stance Points (placing them at the top of the `STUMBLING` bracket) and immediately pass their turn.
*   **"ALL AP" Actions**: `TRIP` and `GET_UP` have been integrated to consume the entire remaining AP pool when executed.

### Reaction Windows
*   **Leftover AP Tracking**: The `CombatTurnManager` banks any unspent AP a unit leaves at the end of their active turn into a `reserved_ap` pool.
*   **Signal Architecture**: Actions like `STRIKE` and `SHOOT` now trigger `reaction_window_opened` before damage resolution. The AI (and soon, your player UI) evaluates this signal to select `BLOCK` or `DODGE` using their banked AP.
*   **The Hazard & Cripple Clauses**: A successful `DODGE` triggers a `check_dodge_hazard` if executed in `MUD`. Furthermore, `DODGE` is completely locked out if both leg limb HPs drop to 0.

### The Firearm Pipelines
*   **Pistol**: `ItemData.gd` now tracks `current_magazine` and `max_magazine`. The `SHOOT` pipeline checks this, and `RELOAD` (2 AP) must be called to refill it from backpack ammo.
*   **Rifle**: Uses the `needs_cycling` flag. `SHOOT` consumes loose ammo directly from the backpack and sets this flag. `CYCLE` (1 AP) must be spent before firing again.
*   **Burst Fire (Reaction)**: The `execute_burst_fire` logic is ready to be hooked up as a Reaction Strike. It automatically converts all remaining reserved AP into shots (4 AP each), applying the strict 2 Stance Points of recoil damage to the shooter per round.

### Grapples and Displacements
*   **Grapple / Charge Intercept**: The `CombatLaneManager` actively intercepts `CHARGE` movements. If a charger ends exactly 1 cell away from an enemy, that enemy can spend 6 reserved AP to force a `GRAPPLE`, tackling the charger into their slot, setting both to 0 Stance (`FELLED`), and opening an `EXECUTE` window.
*   **Leverage Formula**: `PUSH` actions now check $\Delta\text{Stance}$ and Brawn. Failure causes the initiator to slip (2 Stance damage).
*   **Displacement Choices**: A successful push halts execution to fire the `displacement_choice_opened` signal, allowing the initiator to select `STAY` (break lock) or `FOLLOW` (maintain lock).
*   **Braced State (Shove Cushion)**: The Lane Manager prevents forced displacement if a friendly unit is anchored behind the target, dealing blunt trauma to both units instead while keeping the front line locked.

## AI Updates

The `CombatAIEvaluator.gd` cognitive loop was rewritten to utilize the new actions.
- The AI will evaluate remaining AP and try to `RELOAD` or `CYCLE` guns when necessary.
- The AI will spend its `reserved_ap` on `BLOCK` or `DODGE` when attacked off-turn.
- The AI uses its equipped weapon to decide whether to `FOLLOW` (if holding a melee weapon) or `STAY` (if holding a rifle/pistol) after a successful push.

You are now ready to hook the new signals (`reaction_window_opened`, `reaction_resolved`, `displacement_choice_opened`) directly into your visual presentation and animation layer.
